1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,611
Welcome.

2
00:00:00,611 --> 00:00:04,124
Today I want to remove some of the magic without removing the wonder.

3
00:00:04,124 --> 00:00:13,443
We will begin with a single artificial neuron, build up to convolution and transformers, then open current video systems and finally assemble a film workflow you can run and critique.

4
00:00:13,443 --> 00:00:16,701
By the end, a model demo should no longer look like a black box.

5
00:00:16,701 --> 00:00:22,608
You should be able to point to the representation, conditioning, sampling choice, and production decision behind it.

6
00:00:22,608 --> 00:00:25,663
Let us start with the contract for how this class will work.

7
00:00:26,563 --> 00:00:29,006
Model names will change faster than our semester.

8
00:00:29,006 --> 00:00:38,826
The useful skill is learning how to ask stable questions: what is represented, what is predicted, what conditions the prediction, what remains consistent over time, and where human judgment enters.

9
00:00:38,826 --> 00:00:45,755
If Seedance or Mini Max changes next month, those questions still let you diagnose the new system in minutes instead of starting from zero.

10
00:00:45,755 --> 00:00:49,394
Before the technical journey, here is exactly how the course is assessed.

11
00:00:50,294 --> 00:00:53,251
The lecture is deliberately paced as one idea per slide.

12
00:00:53,251 --> 00:00:55,152
The first half builds the machinery.

13
00:00:55,152 --> 00:01:01,542
The second half uses that machinery to read current systems and then convert model capability into a production pipeline.

14
00:01:01,542 --> 00:01:08,090
Think of it like a camera department tour: we first learn the parts, then compare camera bodies, and finally plan the shoot.

15
00:01:08,090 --> 00:01:10,942
A short assessment slide now prevents confusion later.

16
00:01:11,842 --> 00:01:13,752
The course assessment has four parts.

17
00:01:13,752 --> 00:01:16,074
Weekly presentations account for ten percent.

18
00:01:16,074 --> 00:01:18,036
The Lecture 01 quiz is twenty percent.

19
00:01:18,036 --> 00:01:22,165
The three assignments are fifteen percent each, for forty-five percent in total.

20
00:01:22,165 --> 00:01:24,539
The final presentation is twenty-five percent.

21
00:01:24,539 --> 00:01:27,327
Together, these components add to one hundred percent.

22
00:01:27,327 --> 00:01:35,430
The balance rewards two kinds of learning: explaining technical work clearly and developing a sustained creative practice through projects and presentations.

23
00:01:35,430 --> 00:01:38,527
The quiz focuses on the concepts that organize this lecture.

24
00:01:39,427 --> 00:01:42,560
Quiz 01 contains five short responses worth four points each.

25
00:01:42,560 --> 00:01:47,184
A strong answer defines the idea, explains why it matters, and gives one specific example.

26
00:01:47,184 --> 00:01:51,190
The questions are designed to test understanding, not vocabulary memorization.

27
00:01:51,190 --> 00:02:01,721
You will explain the difference between a world and a sequence of images, trace the four-stage generative map, discuss temporal consistency, design a controlled comparison, and critique one recent reading.

28
00:02:01,721 --> 00:02:06,344
The assignment briefs will be announced separately, while the deadlines are already fixed.

29
00:02:07,244 --> 00:02:10,321
The three project briefs will be announced separately.

30
00:02:10,321 --> 00:02:17,673
Their deadlines are fixed: Assignment 1 is due September 30, Assignment 2 is due October 31, and Assignment 3 is due November 30.

31
00:02:17,673 --> 00:02:19,839
Each deadline is 23:59 Hong Kong time.

32
00:02:19,839 --> 00:02:22,289
Treat these dates as production milestones.

33
00:02:22,289 --> 00:02:28,045
Reserve time for iteration, editing, and documentation instead of planning only for the final export.

34
00:02:28,045 --> 00:02:32,490
With the course structure clear, we can begin with the central technical idea.

35
00:02:33,390 --> 00:02:37,098
A video model does not store a shelf of finished films and retrieve one.

36
00:02:37,098 --> 00:02:45,029
It learns statistical regularities in images, motion, language, and sound, then produces one trajectory that is compatible with the conditions we provide.

37
00:02:45,029 --> 00:02:52,497
The practical consequence is important: directing AI video means controlling a process of change, not decorating a still image with motion words.

38
00:02:52,497 --> 00:02:55,896
To understand that process, we need to start with learning itself.

39
00:02:56,796 --> 00:02:57,956
Data supplies examples.

40
00:02:57,956 --> 00:03:01,535
Representation decides what information becomes compact and comparable.

41
00:03:01,535 --> 00:03:04,257
The model learns relationships in that representation.

42
00:03:04,257 --> 00:03:07,534
Sampling turns a learned distribution into one particular output.

43
00:03:07,534 --> 00:03:11,769
This four-stage map will reappear when we inspect Seedance, Mini Max, and Comfy U I.

44
00:03:11,769 --> 00:03:19,432
When a face drifts, we can ask whether the cause is missing data, weak identity representation, insufficient temporal modeling, or an inference setting.

45
00:03:19,432 --> 00:03:21,902
First: what does a neural network actually learn?

46
00:03:22,802 --> 00:03:25,797
This section is intentionally basic, but not simplistic.

47
00:03:25,797 --> 00:03:29,916
We will build enough detail that later architecture diagrams become readable.

48
00:03:29,916 --> 00:03:37,565
You do not need to calculate gradients by hand; you do need to understand what information moves through the network and what training changes.

49
00:03:37,565 --> 00:03:44,197
Once you see networks as transformations of representation, the leap from C N Ns to video transformers becomes much smaller.

50
00:03:44,197 --> 00:03:46,283
Begin with the smallest trainable unit.

51
00:03:47,183 --> 00:03:52,604
Artificial intelligence includes hand-written rules, search procedures, learned predictors, and generative systems.

52
00:03:52,604 --> 00:03:58,071
Modern video models sit mainly in the last two categories: they learn from examples and then synthesize new samples.

53
00:03:58,071 --> 00:04:04,623
A chess engine and a video diffusion model are both called AI, but they represent problems and produce answers in radically different ways.

54
00:04:04,623 --> 00:04:08,017
Machine learning narrows the question to how behavior changes from data.

55
00:04:08,917 --> 00:04:11,335
A supervised training step is simple to state.

56
00:04:11,335 --> 00:04:18,696
Give the network an input, produce a prediction, compare it with a target using a loss, and adjust parameters so future predictions improve.

57
00:04:18,696 --> 00:04:23,743
Generative training uses more elaborate targets, but the optimization loop remains recognizable.

58
00:04:23,743 --> 00:04:30,051
For image classification the target may be ‘cat.’ For diffusion, the target may be the noise that was added to a latent.

59
00:04:30,051 --> 00:04:33,048
The adjustable parameters live inside neurons and layers.

60
00:04:33,948 --> 00:04:40,683
A neuron multiplies each input by a weight, adds the results and a bias, then passes the value through a nonlinear activation.

61
00:04:40,683 --> 00:04:43,356
The weights express which input directions matter.

62
00:04:43,356 --> 00:04:48,114
The nonlinearity lets stacked layers represent more than one large linear transformation.

63
00:04:48,114 --> 00:04:54,689
A single neuron can act like a soft detector: is there enough evidence for a vertical edge, a smile, or a lighting pattern?

64
00:04:54,689 --> 00:04:57,736
Real networks learn thousands of such detectors together.

65
00:04:57,736 --> 00:05:00,355
Training is the process that tunes those weights.

66
00:05:01,255 --> 00:05:04,046
During the forward pass, data moves through the network.

67
00:05:04,046 --> 00:05:06,089
The loss turns performance into a scalar.

68
00:05:06,089 --> 00:05:10,675
Backpropagation uses the chain rule to estimate how each parameter contributed to that loss.

69
00:05:10,675 --> 00:05:14,811
An optimizer then nudges parameters in a direction expected to reduce future error.

70
00:05:14,811 --> 00:05:21,490
No engineer specifies the exact edge filter in a modern network; the filter emerges because it helps reduce loss across many examples.

71
00:05:21,490 --> 00:05:24,829
Stacking neurons creates representations of increasing abstraction.

72
00:05:25,729 --> 00:05:35,524
The familiar story is that early visual layers respond to simple edges, middle layers combine them into textures or parts, and deeper layers respond to larger semantic patterns.

73
00:05:35,524 --> 00:05:39,398
The hierarchy is not perfectly clean, but it is a useful mental model.

74
00:05:39,398 --> 00:05:43,271
A face detector does not need a separate rule for every possible face.

75
00:05:43,271 --> 00:05:47,809
It learns reusable intermediate features such as contours, eyes, and arrangements.

76
00:05:47,809 --> 00:05:50,742
Convolution made this hierarchy practical for images.

77
00:05:51,642 --> 00:05:53,940
An image is not an arbitrary list of numbers.

78
00:05:53,940 --> 00:05:59,559
Neighbouring pixels are correlated, patterns repeat across locations, and the same object can appear anywhere.

79
00:05:59,559 --> 00:06:04,615
A good architecture should exploit those regularities instead of relearning them at every position.

80
00:06:04,615 --> 00:06:09,007
Convolution encodes that prior directly: look locally with the same filter everywhere.

81
00:06:09,007 --> 00:06:11,408
Let us make the convolution operation tangible.

82
00:06:12,308 --> 00:06:14,428
A small kernel slides across the image.

83
00:06:14,428 --> 00:06:17,744
At each location it computes a weighted sum of nearby pixels.

84
00:06:17,744 --> 00:06:21,169
The result is a feature map showing where that pattern appears.

85
00:06:21,169 --> 00:06:25,300
During training, the kernel values are learned rather than designed by hand.

86
00:06:25,300 --> 00:06:27,692
One filter may respond to vertical contrast.

87
00:06:27,692 --> 00:06:29,866
Another may respond to a colored corner.

88
00:06:29,866 --> 00:06:33,182
Deeper filters combine earlier maps into more complex motifs.

89
00:06:33,182 --> 00:06:35,139
The key advantage is weight sharing.

90
00:06:36,039 --> 00:06:38,229
Kernel size controls the local window.

91
00:06:38,229 --> 00:06:40,592
Stride controls how far the filter moves.

92
00:06:40,592 --> 00:06:43,070
Padding controls what happens near borders.

93
00:06:43,070 --> 00:06:47,910
Channels let the filter combine information across RGB or intermediate feature maps.

94
00:06:47,910 --> 00:06:56,612
A three-by-three kernel with stride one preserves fine local detail; larger strides reduce resolution and computation but may discard small structures.

95
00:06:56,612 --> 00:06:59,148
These choices determine the receptive field.

96
00:07:00,048 --> 00:07:03,374
After convolution, each output channel is a spatial map of activations.

97
00:07:03,374 --> 00:07:05,998
It is useful to imagine a channel as a learned question.

98
00:07:05,998 --> 00:07:10,120
The network asks that question at every location and records the strength of the answer.

99
00:07:10,120 --> 00:07:12,181
Later layers do not see raw pixels directly.

100
00:07:12,181 --> 00:07:16,210
They see stacks of these learned answers, which is why representation matters so much.

101
00:07:16,210 --> 00:07:19,349
As layers stack, each activation sees a larger region of the image.

102
00:07:20,249 --> 00:07:24,543
The receptive field is the portion of the input that can influence one activation.

103
00:07:24,543 --> 00:07:26,166
Stacking convolutions grows it.

104
00:07:26,166 --> 00:07:31,140
Early units see small patches; later units can integrate information across an object or scene.

105
00:07:31,140 --> 00:07:37,790
A hand can be rendered sharply yet attach to the wrong arm if the model captures local texture better than long-range relation.

106
00:07:37,790 --> 00:07:41,036
Downsampling expands context, but it also creates a trade-off.

107
00:07:41,936 --> 00:07:44,454
Pooling or strided convolution reduces spatial size.

108
00:07:44,454 --> 00:07:47,796
This makes deeper processing cheaper and increases effective context.

109
00:07:47,796 --> 00:07:53,269
But information thrown away early is difficult to reconstruct later, especially text, fingers, and thin geometry.

110
00:07:53,269 --> 00:07:58,160
Many generative systems therefore work at multiple scales or use skip connections to preserve detail.

111
00:07:58,160 --> 00:08:01,841
Residual connections solve a different problem: training very deep networks.

112
00:08:02,741 --> 00:08:06,778
A residual block adds its learned transformation back to the original input.

113
00:08:06,778 --> 00:08:12,303
If the new transformation is temporarily unhelpful, the identity path still carries information forward.

114
00:08:12,303 --> 00:08:14,906
This makes very deep networks easier to optimize.

115
00:08:14,906 --> 00:08:21,069
The same idea appears throughout modern architectures: preserve a stable path, then learn a useful update around it.

116
00:08:21,069 --> 00:08:25,850
C N Ns were transformative, but their local bias becomes awkward for global relationships.

117
00:08:26,750 --> 00:08:29,457
C N Ns are excellent at exploiting locality and grids.

118
00:08:29,457 --> 00:08:37,127
Their weakness is not that they cannot model global structure, but that global interaction is indirect and often requires many layers or special modules.

119
00:08:37,127 --> 00:08:40,987
In video, a character may disappear behind a wall and reappear seconds later.

120
00:08:40,987 --> 00:08:44,947
Local filters alone do not naturally express that long-range identity relation.

121
00:08:44,947 --> 00:08:47,955
Sequence models approach the problem from another direction.

122
00:08:48,855 --> 00:08:58,771
Attention begins with a provocative idea: instead of deciding in advance which neighbours matter, let each element compare itself with all relevant elements and construct a weighted mixture.

123
00:08:58,771 --> 00:09:04,147
That flexible relation mechanism is why transformers moved from language into images, audio, and video.

124
00:09:04,147 --> 00:09:06,391
We need one more abstraction first: tokens.

125
00:09:07,291 --> 00:09:09,289
A token is a unit the model processes.

126
00:09:09,289 --> 00:09:11,288
In language it may be a word fragment.

127
00:09:11,288 --> 00:09:13,286
In vision it may be a patch embedding.

128
00:09:13,286 --> 00:09:16,810
In video it may represent a compressed block across space and time.

129
00:09:16,810 --> 00:09:21,017
Once converted to vectors, very different media can share transformer machinery.

130
00:09:21,017 --> 00:09:22,648
The token is not the raw thing.

131
00:09:22,648 --> 00:09:27,065
It is a learned representation designed to preserve information useful for the task.

132
00:09:27,065 --> 00:09:30,168
Embeddings place those tokens in a continuous vector space.

133
00:09:31,068 --> 00:09:34,090
An embedding maps a discrete or structured input into a vector.

134
00:09:34,090 --> 00:09:39,943
The coordinates do not have simple names, but distances and directions can capture useful relationships learned from data.

135
00:09:39,943 --> 00:09:47,666
A text prompt, a reference image, and an audio cue may enter through different encoders, yet all must eventually become representations the generator can relate.

136
00:09:47,666 --> 00:09:50,880
Attention performs that relation through queries, keys, and values.

137
00:09:51,780 --> 00:09:54,083
Each token produces a query, a key, and a value.

138
00:09:54,083 --> 00:09:56,483
Query-key similarity determines attention weights.

139
00:09:56,483 --> 00:09:59,026
The weighted sum of values becomes the updated token.

140
00:09:59,026 --> 00:10:03,441
The square-root scaling prevents dot products from becoming so large that softmax saturates.

141
00:10:03,441 --> 00:10:09,440
For a red scarf across frames, the current token can attend to earlier tokens that carry the scarf’s identity and appearance.

142
00:10:09,440 --> 00:10:12,799
Multiple heads let the model learn several relationship types at once.

143
00:10:13,699 --> 00:10:18,310
Instead of one attention map, multi-head attention projects tokens into several subspaces.

144
00:10:18,310 --> 00:10:23,228
One head may emphasize local appearance, another long-distance identity, another temporal order.

145
00:10:23,228 --> 00:10:25,226
The outputs are concatenated and mixed.

146
00:10:25,226 --> 00:10:32,500
Heads are not guaranteed to align with neat human concepts, but the parallel structure expands the kinds of relations one layer can represent.

147
00:10:32,500 --> 00:10:36,855
The transformer block wraps attention with normalization, an MLP, and residual paths.

148
00:10:37,755 --> 00:10:42,006
The original transformer used an encoder and decoder, each built from repeated blocks.

149
00:10:42,006 --> 00:10:51,595
Modern vision and diffusion systems modify this structure, but the recurring pattern remains: mix information across tokens, transform each token with an MLP, and preserve stable residual paths.

150
00:10:51,595 --> 00:10:55,994
The architecture diagram looks complicated until you recognize those repeated operations.

151
00:10:55,994 --> 00:10:58,960
A sequence still needs information about order and position.

152
00:10:59,860 --> 00:11:06,152
Because attention compares a set of vectors, it needs extra structure to distinguish left from right or earlier from later.

153
00:11:06,152 --> 00:11:08,095
Position encodings inject coordinates.

154
00:11:08,095 --> 00:11:13,824
Rotary position embedding, or RoPE, rotates query and key components so relative position influences similarity.

155
00:11:13,824 --> 00:11:17,456
In video, useful position may have three axes: time, height, and width.

156
00:11:17,456 --> 00:11:21,753
Mini Max H three and Hunyuan Video explicitly describe spatiotemporal RoPE variants.

157
00:11:21,753 --> 00:11:24,873
Vision Transformers apply the sequence idea to image patches.

158
00:11:25,773 --> 00:11:35,301
A Vision Transformer cuts an image into patches, flattens or projects each patch into a token, adds position information, and processes the sequence with transformer blocks.

159
00:11:35,301 --> 00:11:39,651
It replaces much of the C N N’s local bias with data-driven global interaction.

160
00:11:39,651 --> 00:11:45,269
The price is token count: smaller patches preserve detail but create longer, more expensive sequences.

161
00:11:45,269 --> 00:11:49,950
Diffusion Transformers use related blocks to predict how noisy latents should change.

162
00:11:50,850 --> 00:12:00,837
diffusion transformer patchifies a noisy latent, adds timestep and conditioning information, processes the tokens through transformer blocks, and reconstructs a prediction in latent space.

163
00:12:00,837 --> 00:12:06,202
The paper showed that increasing transformer compute correlated with better image generation quality.

164
00:12:06,202 --> 00:12:12,523
This architecture scales naturally to long token sequences, which helped make it a foundation for modern video systems.

165
00:12:12,523 --> 00:12:16,560
Before diffusion, we need to understand why models generate in latent space.

166
00:12:17,460 --> 00:12:20,145
Raw video contains an enormous number of values.

167
00:12:20,145 --> 00:12:27,135
A practical generator usually compresses frames into latents, performs expensive modeling there, and decodes only at the end.

168
00:12:27,135 --> 00:12:29,373
The compression is not just engineering.

169
00:12:29,373 --> 00:12:33,623
It defines which differences the model can preserve, ignore, or reconstruct.

170
00:12:33,623 --> 00:12:35,972
An autoencoder provides the basic pattern.

171
00:12:36,872 --> 00:12:40,282
The encoder maps an input to a smaller latent representation.

172
00:12:40,282 --> 00:12:43,413
The decoder reconstructs the input from that bottleneck.

173
00:12:43,413 --> 00:12:49,003
Training rewards faithful reconstruction, so the latent must preserve information the decoder needs.

174
00:12:49,003 --> 00:12:53,195
If the bottleneck is too narrow, small text and identity details disappear.

175
00:12:53,195 --> 00:12:57,109
If it is too wide, the later generative model faces a costly sequence.

176
00:12:57,109 --> 00:13:00,910
A variational autoencoder adds structure to the latent distribution.

177
00:13:01,810 --> 00:13:06,735
A V A E predicts parameters of a latent distribution rather than a single deterministic code.

178
00:13:06,735 --> 00:13:10,018
A regularization term encourages nearby, sampleable structure.

179
00:13:10,018 --> 00:13:15,207
This makes the latent space more suitable for generation, though reconstruction can become softer.

180
00:13:15,207 --> 00:13:23,521
In modern video systems, the term V A E often refers broadly to a learned compressor-decoder, even when architectural details differ from the original V A E.

181
00:13:23,521 --> 00:13:27,334
Latent diffusion moves the denoising process into this compressed space.

182
00:13:28,234 --> 00:13:31,982
Latent diffusion reduces the spatial size before running the generative model.

183
00:13:31,982 --> 00:13:36,930
This lowers memory and compute while retaining enough perceptual information for high-quality decoding.

184
00:13:36,930 --> 00:13:39,333
Text or other conditions guide the denoising path.

185
00:13:39,333 --> 00:13:44,473
The idea is central to video: without spatial and temporal compression, token sequences become prohibitive.

186
00:13:44,473 --> 00:13:47,164
Diffusion itself begins by deliberately corrupting data.

187
00:13:48,064 --> 00:13:51,629
The forward process gradually adds Gaussian noise according to a schedule.

188
00:13:51,629 --> 00:13:58,758
During training, we can jump directly to a chosen noise level, ask the network to predict the added noise or a related target, and compute an error.

189
00:13:58,758 --> 00:14:04,683
The network sees examples ranging from almost clean to almost pure noise, learning useful directions across the whole path.

190
00:14:04,683 --> 00:14:06,947
Generation runs the learned process in reverse.

191
00:14:07,847 --> 00:14:14,082
At inference, a sampler starts with random noise and repeatedly applies the model’s prediction according to a numerical schedule.

192
00:14:14,082 --> 00:14:18,190
Each step moves the latent toward a sample compatible with training and conditioning.

193
00:14:18,190 --> 00:14:24,811
This is why seeds matter: the initial noise chooses a starting point, but the prompt, references, guidance, and scheduler shape the path.

194
00:14:24,811 --> 00:14:27,566
The training target can be parameterized in several ways.

195
00:14:28,466 --> 00:14:32,959
A diffusion model may predict noise, the clean sample, or a velocity-like combination.

196
00:14:32,959 --> 00:14:39,437
These parameterizations affect optimization and numerical behavior, even when they describe related generative trajectories.

197
00:14:39,437 --> 00:14:44,975
Product interfaces hide this choice, while open workflows may expose model type and scheduler assumptions.

198
00:14:44,975 --> 00:14:47,849
A mismatch can produce poor contrast, color, or motion.

199
00:14:47,849 --> 00:14:51,297
Sampling quality also depends on how many steps we take and where.

200
00:14:52,197 --> 00:14:55,389
A sampler is a numerical method for following the reverse process.

201
00:14:55,389 --> 00:14:57,808
The schedule allocates effort across noise levels.

202
00:14:57,808 --> 00:15:03,371
Distilled models may work in very few steps because training has compressed a longer trajectory into a shorter one.

203
00:15:03,371 --> 00:15:06,418
Do not assume more steps always improve a distilled checkpoint.

204
00:15:06,418 --> 00:15:09,901
Follow the model’s recommended regime, then test one variable at a time.

205
00:15:09,901 --> 00:15:12,706
Conditioning steers which solution the trajectory reaches.

206
00:15:13,606 --> 00:15:19,268
Classifier-free guidance compares a conditional and an unconditional prediction, then amplifies the difference.

207
00:15:19,268 --> 00:15:25,032
Stronger guidance can improve prompt adherence but may reduce diversity or create oversaturated, brittle results.

208
00:15:25,032 --> 00:15:31,357
In video, excessive guidance can also harm motion because every frame is forced too aggressively toward static textual cues.

209
00:15:31,357 --> 00:15:34,672
Flow matching offers another view of the same generative journey.

210
00:15:35,572 --> 00:15:41,747
Flow matching trains a vector field that transports samples from a simple distribution toward the data distribution.

211
00:15:41,747 --> 00:15:44,674
At inference, an ODE solver follows that learned field.

212
00:15:44,674 --> 00:15:48,081
Rectified-flow variants aim for straighter, easier trajectories.

213
00:15:48,081 --> 00:15:53,883
Many current models use flow-style objectives while keeping transformer backbones and latent representations.

214
00:15:53,883 --> 00:15:58,887
The labels change, but representation, conditioning, and numerical integration remain central.

215
00:15:58,887 --> 00:16:01,282
Now extend those ideas from images into time.

216
00:16:02,182 --> 00:16:04,430
Video is not an image with more frames.

217
00:16:04,430 --> 00:16:10,712
The model must preserve identity, geometry, lighting, and world state while also producing meaningful change.

218
00:16:10,712 --> 00:16:15,150
It must decide what moves, when it moves, and how one event affects the next.

219
00:16:15,150 --> 00:16:19,876
This is the technical reason a beautiful first frame can still become a weak shot.

220
00:16:19,876 --> 00:16:22,412
Start by counting what a raw video contains.

221
00:16:23,312 --> 00:16:26,758
A video can be represented as a T-by-H-by-W-by-C tensor.

222
00:16:26,758 --> 00:16:28,788
Doubling duration doubles values.

223
00:16:28,788 --> 00:16:31,803
Doubling both spatial dimensions quadruples them.

224
00:16:31,803 --> 00:16:38,756
Attention over all raw locations would grow even faster because pairwise interaction is quadratic in token count.

225
00:16:38,756 --> 00:16:43,556
A ten-second 24-fps clip already contains 240 frames before audio is included.

226
00:16:43,556 --> 00:16:48,232
Compression is therefore a design requirement, not an optional optimization.

227
00:16:49,132 --> 00:16:53,303
A video V A E compresses height and width and often reduces the time axis as well.

228
00:16:53,303 --> 00:16:58,694
Causal designs ensure a latent does not depend on future frames in ways that break streaming or extension.

229
00:16:58,694 --> 00:17:05,714
Mini Max H three reports sixteen-times spatial and four-times temporal V A E compression before an additional spatial patchification step.

230
00:17:05,714 --> 00:17:08,155
The resulting latent blocks become video tokens.

231
00:17:09,055 --> 00:17:11,758
Patchification groups latent values into tokens.

232
00:17:11,758 --> 00:17:14,236
Each token carries content plus coordinates.

233
00:17:14,236 --> 00:17:18,573
The transformer then models relationships across space, time, and conditions.

234
00:17:18,573 --> 00:17:22,121
Larger patches reduce cost but risk losing fine motion or text.

235
00:17:22,121 --> 00:17:26,176
Smaller patches preserve detail but increase sequence length and memory.

236
00:17:26,176 --> 00:17:30,400
Temporal attention determines how broadly tokens communicate across frames.

237
00:17:31,300 --> 00:17:37,824
Full spatiotemporal attention provides maximum flexibility but becomes expensive as duration and resolution grow.

238
00:17:37,824 --> 00:17:44,926
Sparse, sliding, factorized, or block attention limits which tokens interact, trading some flexibility for practical scale.

239
00:17:44,926 --> 00:17:55,723
Hunyuan Video 1.5 uses selective and sliding tile attention; Mini Max H three describes native sparse-attention support, although its first open release uses full attention for inference.

240
00:17:55,723 --> 00:18:00,400
Longer context is useful only if the representation preserves identity and state.

241
00:18:01,300 --> 00:18:04,481
A multimodal generator has a finite context and compute budget.

242
00:18:04,481 --> 00:18:10,541
More reference files, higher resolution, longer duration, and native audio increase the sequence or conditioning burden.

243
00:18:10,541 --> 00:18:20,540
The practical question is not ‘how many references can I upload?’ but ‘which references provide non-redundant control?’ A clear hero image and motion reference may outperform twelve ambiguous files.

244
00:18:20,540 --> 00:18:22,459
That leads to multimodal conditioning.

245
00:18:23,359 --> 00:18:25,484
Text-to-video invents nearly everything.

246
00:18:25,484 --> 00:18:27,291
Image-to-video anchors appearance.

247
00:18:27,291 --> 00:18:30,001
First-and-last-frame workflows constrain endpoints.

248
00:18:30,001 --> 00:18:33,508
Reference-to-video can transfer identity, style, motion, or sound.

249
00:18:33,508 --> 00:18:38,130
Editing workflows preserve more of an existing clip while changing selected attributes.

250
00:18:38,130 --> 00:18:40,947
Choose the task with the least unnecessary invention.

251
00:18:40,947 --> 00:18:44,400
If identity already exists, do not ask text alone to recreate it.

252
00:18:44,400 --> 00:18:48,651
Audio is becoming a condition and an output rather than a separate afterthought.

253
00:18:49,551 --> 00:18:53,478
Native audio-video models predict related visual and audio latents.

254
00:18:53,478 --> 00:19:04,496
Synchronization is not one problem: lip motion may require frame-level alignment, footsteps need event timing, ambience needs scene consistency, and music operates across longer structure.

255
00:19:04,496 --> 00:19:08,657
A clip can have excellent sound quality and poor audiovisual causality.

256
00:19:08,657 --> 00:19:10,826
Evaluate both fidelity and alignment.

257
00:19:10,826 --> 00:19:14,518
Before training, the system needs usable data and descriptions.

258
00:19:15,418 --> 00:19:21,583
Video training data contains duplicates, cuts, overlays, watermarks, weak motion, missing audio, and uncertain rights.

259
00:19:21,583 --> 00:19:24,195
Curation filters and scores clips before training.

260
00:19:24,195 --> 00:19:28,740
The selection criteria silently define which kinds of worlds become easy for the model.

261
00:19:28,740 --> 00:19:35,689
A dataset dominated by polished advertising will make glossy product shots easier than under-documented local performance traditions.

262
00:19:35,689 --> 00:19:38,667
Captions turn raw clips into controllable training pairs.

263
00:19:39,567 --> 00:19:48,301
A weak caption says ‘a person outside.’ A production-grade caption can describe subject, action, shot size, camera movement, lighting, sound, and temporal order.

264
00:19:48,301 --> 00:19:52,369
Some systems train caption models and refiners specifically for video data.

265
00:19:52,369 --> 00:19:59,529
Hunyuan Video 1.5 describes a post-training pipeline that improves bilingual and glyph-aware descriptions before generator training.

266
00:19:59,529 --> 00:20:03,001
Pretraining creates capability; post-training shapes usefulness.

267
00:20:03,901 --> 00:20:10,645
After large-scale pretraining, teams refine instruction following, aesthetic preference, motion quality, safety, and speed.

268
00:20:10,645 --> 00:20:15,688
Seedance 1.0 reports supervised fine-tuning and video R L H F with multidimensional rewards.

269
00:20:15,688 --> 00:20:18,485
Distillation compresses inference into fewer steps.

270
00:20:18,485 --> 00:20:26,325
The product you use is therefore not only an architecture; it is an architecture plus data, post-training, safety policy, and inference system.

271
00:20:26,325 --> 00:20:28,682
Evaluation tries to separate those effects.

272
00:20:29,582 --> 00:20:33,264
Benchmarks combine automated metrics, multimodal judges, and human ratings.

273
00:20:33,264 --> 00:20:34,295
Each has blind spots.

274
00:20:34,295 --> 00:20:39,548
A model may score well on average yet fail the exact identity, camera, or causal requirement of your scene.

275
00:20:39,548 --> 00:20:49,269
For production, add task-specific acceptance tests: does the hand complete the action, does the logo remain readable, does the sound occur at contact, and is the usable interval long enough to edit?

276
00:20:49,269 --> 00:20:51,577
A failure vocabulary makes those tests precise.

277
00:20:52,477 --> 00:20:55,039
Semantic failures change what the scene means.

278
00:20:55,039 --> 00:20:57,489
Spatial failures break geometry and contact.

279
00:20:57,489 --> 00:20:59,439
Temporal failures flicker or drift.

280
00:20:59,439 --> 00:21:02,000
Physical failures violate motion or causality.

281
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,618
Identity failures change a subject across time.

282
00:21:04,618 --> 00:21:09,574
Aesthetic failure can overlay all five when the result contradicts the intended language.

283
00:21:09,574 --> 00:21:12,192
‘Bad consistency’ is too vague to choose a fix.

284
00:21:12,192 --> 00:21:17,483
‘Identity changes after occlusion’ suggests a reference, duration, or shot-design intervention.

285
00:21:17,483 --> 00:21:20,880
Now place these mechanisms in the current research landscape.

286
00:21:21,780 --> 00:21:33,537
The 2026 survey frames the field as an evolution from adversarial learning to diffusion and autoregressive approaches, with current systems converging toward unified multimodal foundation models.

287
00:21:33,537 --> 00:21:37,817
The categories are not clean product boxes; many systems combine ideas.

288
00:21:37,817 --> 00:21:45,052
The useful reading is architectural: what probability path is learned, how media is tokenized, and how conditions enter.

289
00:21:45,052 --> 00:21:48,187
A taxonomy shows the major branches more concretely.

290
00:21:49,087 --> 00:21:53,508
The survey maps modern video generation across GAN, diffusion, and autoregressive branches.

291
00:21:53,508 --> 00:22:00,891
The dominant frontier currently uses diffusion or flow with transformer backbones, while token-based autoregressive and hybrid systems continue to grow.

292
00:22:00,891 --> 00:22:02,932
Do not read the diagram as a winner board.

293
00:22:02,932 --> 00:22:07,790
Read it as a menu of trade-offs in likelihood, sequence length, sampling speed, and controllability.

294
00:22:07,790 --> 00:22:10,850
The timeline reveals how quickly the capability frontier moved.

295
00:22:11,750 --> 00:22:14,436
The survey’s timeline shows the problem changing.

296
00:22:14,436 --> 00:22:16,738
Early systems competed on coherent motion.

297
00:22:16,738 --> 00:22:23,591
Later work emphasized stability and control, then physical grounding, multimodal integration, and longer narrative structure.

298
00:22:23,591 --> 00:22:30,662
That shift explains why current releases foreground references, editing, sound, and story continuity rather than only resolution.

299
00:22:30,662 --> 00:22:34,116
A compact comparison will help us choose models for production.

300
00:22:35,016 --> 00:22:39,647
Marketing comparisons often mix resolution, duration, task, and human preference.

301
00:22:39,647 --> 00:22:50,910
A more technical comparison asks how media is compressed, which network predicts latents, what conditions are supported, what the output contains, and whether the system is open, hosted, or hybrid.

302
00:22:50,910 --> 00:22:59,600
This framework lets us compare Seedance, Mini Max H three, Wan, and Hunyuan without pretending their published evaluations are directly interchangeable.

303
00:22:59,600 --> 00:23:01,887
We begin with ByteDance’s Seedance line.

304
00:23:02,787 --> 00:23:06,662
Seedance is best understood as a sequence of system-level changes.

305
00:23:06,662 --> 00:23:09,715
Version 1.0 established a scalable video foundation.

306
00:23:09,715 --> 00:23:12,768
Version 1.5 Pro added native audio-video generation.

307
00:23:12,768 --> 00:23:15,704
Version 2.0 unified multimodal inputs and editing.

308
00:23:15,704 --> 00:23:18,639
Version 2.5 extends duration and one-take control.

309
00:23:18,639 --> 00:23:24,100
Because 2.5 is proprietary, we must separate reported capability from disclosed architecture.

310
00:23:24,100 --> 00:23:26,918
Start with what the 1.0 report actually reveals.

311
00:23:27,818 --> 00:23:31,085
The 1.0 report emphasizes that architecture alone was not enough.

312
00:23:31,085 --> 00:23:34,704
Multi-source curation and precise video captioning improved supervision.

313
00:23:34,704 --> 00:23:37,268
One model handled text-to-video and image-to-video.

314
00:23:37,268 --> 00:23:42,847
Post-training used multidimensional reward signals, and engineering plus distillation targeted practical speed.

315
00:23:42,847 --> 00:23:48,477
The report gives a useful production lesson: quality emerges from the whole pipeline, not from one clever block.

316
00:23:48,477 --> 00:23:51,995
Its native multi-shot ability anticipates later storytelling features.

317
00:23:52,895 --> 00:23:56,213
Multi-shot generation means more than producing several pretty clips.

318
00:23:56,213 --> 00:24:00,636
The system must preserve entities and setting while allowing viewpoint and action to change.

319
00:24:00,636 --> 00:24:05,589
That requires modeling relations across shot boundaries rather than only smooth motion within one shot.

320
00:24:05,589 --> 00:24:11,214
A close-up should feel like the same event as the establishing shot, not a new sample that happens to share keywords.

321
00:24:11,214 --> 00:24:14,147
Seedance 1.5 Pro added a second difficult axis: native sound.

322
00:24:15,047 --> 00:24:19,427
The 1.5 Pro report presents a native audio-visual joint generation foundation model.

323
00:24:19,427 --> 00:24:26,206
Joint generation lets visual events and sound influence a shared process instead of relying entirely on post-hoc audio generation.

324
00:24:26,206 --> 00:24:33,245
For filmmaking, this can accelerate previs and dialogue experiments, but native sound is not a reason to skip deliberate sound editing.

325
00:24:33,245 --> 00:24:36,113
Seedance two point zero expands the input side as well.

326
00:24:37,013 --> 00:24:43,441
Seedance two point zero accepts text, images, audio, and video as multimodal context and supports generation plus editing.

327
00:24:43,441 --> 00:24:50,397
The technical report describes a unified, large-scale architecture but does not publish enough block-level detail to reconstruct it.

328
00:24:50,397 --> 00:24:52,136
That disclosure boundary matters.

329
00:24:52,136 --> 00:24:59,302
We can discuss supported modalities and evaluations confidently, while treating internal tokenization and transformer design as unknown.

330
00:24:59,302 --> 00:25:02,305
The system exposes three especially useful task families.

331
00:25:03,205 --> 00:25:06,589
Text-to-video is appropriate for ideation and broad exploration.

332
00:25:06,589 --> 00:25:10,237
Image-to-video is better when composition or identity already exists.

333
00:25:10,237 --> 00:25:15,735
Reference-to-video can combine images, clips, and audio to specify appearance, motion, rhythm, or voice.

334
00:25:15,735 --> 00:25:20,335
The best mode is often the one that removes degrees of freedom the story does not need.

335
00:25:20,335 --> 00:25:23,296
Reference capacity is not the same as reference clarity.

336
00:25:24,196 --> 00:25:26,747
A clean reference stack gives each file a role.

337
00:25:26,747 --> 00:25:34,509
One image anchors character identity, another defines environment, a short video specifies motion, and audio establishes performance or rhythm.

338
00:25:34,509 --> 00:25:38,309
Redundant or contradictory references make the control problem harder.

339
00:25:38,309 --> 00:25:41,240
Write the role next to every file before uploading it.

340
00:25:41,240 --> 00:25:43,736
If you cannot say what it controls, remove it.

341
00:25:43,736 --> 00:25:48,513
Seedance two point zero reports direct audio-video generation over short shot durations.

342
00:25:49,413 --> 00:25:55,549
The Seedance two point zero evaluation separates audio quality, audio prompt following, and audio-visual synchronization.

343
00:25:55,549 --> 00:25:57,323
That is the right conceptual split.

344
00:25:57,323 --> 00:26:02,698
A beautiful soundtrack can still be wrong if it ignores events or changes the scene’s emotional direction.

345
00:26:02,698 --> 00:26:08,226
For a glass landing on a table, the impact sound should align with contact and reflect the material and room.

346
00:26:08,226 --> 00:26:11,268
Multi-shot and longer-form control are where 2.5 moves next.

347
00:26:12,168 --> 00:26:21,301
ByteDance describes 2.5 as inheriting the unified multimodal audio-video architecture of 2.0 while improving long-form storytelling, flexible references, and editing.

348
00:26:21,301 --> 00:26:25,153
A single generation can reach thirty seconds, with further extensions.

349
00:26:25,153 --> 00:26:28,564
That duration is useful only when the shot has internal beats.

350
00:26:28,564 --> 00:26:34,561
‘Thirty seconds of cinematic motion’ is weaker than a timed sequence of setup, action, reaction, and release.

351
00:26:34,561 --> 00:26:37,367
Prompting should therefore resemble shot direction.

352
00:26:38,267 --> 00:26:44,799
A strong prompt names the starting state, camera behavior, ordered action, environmental consequence, and sound.

353
00:26:44,799 --> 00:26:48,356
Separate fixed constraints from events that change over time.

354
00:26:48,356 --> 00:26:59,320
For example: locked 35mm medium shot; courier enters frame, pauses at the door, hears a distant alarm, turns toward camera; red emergency light sweeps once; room tone and one metal impact.

355
00:26:59,320 --> 00:27:02,119
Use one generation to test one shot proposition.

356
00:27:03,019 --> 00:27:11,984
Seedance is valuable when a team needs to explore an event with multiple reference types, preview audio-video timing, or create a longer shot with internal development.

357
00:27:11,984 --> 00:27:16,894
It is not a replacement for editing, coverage, or continuity management across a whole film.

358
00:27:16,894 --> 00:27:22,497
Treat each output as rushes: inspect, trim, compare, and decide whether it earns a place in the sequence.

359
00:27:22,497 --> 00:27:25,432
The technical unknowns also require disciplined claims.

360
00:27:26,332 --> 00:27:30,193
The reports support claims about modalities, tasks, duration, and evaluation.

361
00:27:30,193 --> 00:27:35,408
They do not fully reveal the 2.5 backbone, parameter count, token packing, or training data composition.

362
00:27:35,408 --> 00:27:39,420
Saying ‘probably a diffusion transformer’ is an inference, not a disclosed fact.

363
00:27:39,420 --> 00:27:42,278
Technical literacy includes knowing where evidence stops.

364
00:27:42,278 --> 00:27:44,935
That makes critique more credible, not less exciting.

365
00:27:44,935 --> 00:27:49,348
Mini Max H three gives us a contrasting case with unusually detailed open documentation.

366
00:27:50,248 --> 00:27:53,906
We now turn to H three, Mini Max’s open audio-video foundation model.

367
00:27:53,906 --> 00:28:07,847
H three is important because the release describes a complete multimodal generation system rather than only a product interface: structured context, a large omni-modal transformer, separate visual and audio latent spaces, and a high-resolution regeneration stage.

368
00:28:07,847 --> 00:28:17,124
The architecture gives us enough detail to connect familiar ideas—encoders, patchification, attention, latent compression, and conditioning—to a contemporary production model.

369
00:28:17,124 --> 00:28:21,577
Begin with the system-level path from the user’s material to the final two K result.

370
00:28:22,477 --> 00:28:27,155
H three-Context I R turns free-form multimodal inputs into a structured intermediate representation.

371
00:28:27,155 --> 00:28:30,664
H three-Base generates synchronized audio and video at seven sixty-eight P.

372
00:28:30,664 --> 00:28:36,278
H three-Regenerate-two K feeds the result and original context back into the model for a higher-resolution regeneration.

373
00:28:36,278 --> 00:28:39,272
Notice that two K is not described as ordinary super-resolution.

374
00:28:39,272 --> 00:28:42,313
The system reuses semantic context while generating detail again.

375
00:28:42,313 --> 00:28:45,354
Context I R is essential, but it is not part of the open weights.

376
00:28:46,254 --> 00:28:51,017
Context I R reasons about how text, images, video, and audio relate to the intended output.

377
00:28:51,017 --> 00:28:56,513
Mini Max says the hosted system may supplement underspecified details without changing the user’s intent.

378
00:28:56,513 --> 00:29:00,544
It distills large source material into a much smaller context representation.

379
00:29:00,544 --> 00:29:08,081
This explains why copying only the final text prompt may fail to reproduce a hosted result: the hidden orchestration step is part of the system.

380
00:29:08,081 --> 00:29:10,803
H three-Base then encodes each modality differently.

381
00:29:11,703 --> 00:29:18,420
The H three encoder uses full pretrained Qwen3-VL-32B weights and passes hidden states from layer fifty to the generator.

382
00:29:18,420 --> 00:29:22,971
Visual inputs also go through the VisualVAE, while audio uses a separate AudioVAE.

383
00:29:22,971 --> 00:29:27,468
One representation carries semantic context; another preserves generative detail.

384
00:29:27,468 --> 00:29:30,077
This dual role is common in multimodal systems.

385
00:29:30,077 --> 00:29:32,630
The visual compressor is unusually aggressive.

386
00:29:33,530 --> 00:29:40,792
H three-VisualVAE is temporally causal with a spatial compression factor of sixteen, temporal factor of four, and twenty-four latent channels.

387
00:29:40,792 --> 00:29:45,701
A one-by-two-by-two patchification further halves each spatial dimension before the transformer.

388
00:29:45,701 --> 00:29:53,934
That creates an effective thirty-two-times spatial downsampling at the token level, dramatically reducing sequence length while demanding a very capable decoder.

389
00:29:53,934 --> 00:29:56,082
Audio follows a separate compression path.

390
00:29:56,982 --> 00:30:05,316
H three-AudioVAE uses the same encoder and decoder parameters for left and right channels while processing them independently, then recombines them as stereo.

391
00:30:05,316 --> 00:30:09,061
Each channel’s 32-kilohertz audio becomes latent tokens at forty hertz.

392
00:30:09,061 --> 00:30:15,128
The generator therefore predicts audio and video latents with very different natural token rates inside one system.

393
00:30:15,128 --> 00:30:18,187
Those tokens are packed into a single multimodal sequence.

394
00:30:19,087 --> 00:30:28,726
H three organizes encoded modalities into one packed sequence, adds rotary position information for spatial and temporal relationships, and passes the result to a single-stream transformer.

395
00:30:28,726 --> 00:30:31,685
The transformer jointly predicts visual and audio latents.

396
00:30:31,685 --> 00:30:38,825
Modality-specific structures are concentrated at input, output, and adaptive normalization branches rather than separate attention networks.

397
00:30:38,825 --> 00:30:41,732
The core transformer is large but architecturally simple.

398
00:30:42,632 --> 00:30:45,856
Mini Max reports a thirty-three-billion-parameter dense transformer.

399
00:30:45,856 --> 00:30:55,337
Roughly thirteen billion parameters belong to adaptive layer-normalization branches whose outputs can be precomputed and cached, so inference-only deployments do not need to load them in the same way.

400
00:30:55,337 --> 00:30:58,181
The attention and feed-forward layers are modality-agnostic.

401
00:30:58,181 --> 00:31:01,832
That simplicity is intended to support many tasks without separate backbones.

402
00:31:01,832 --> 00:31:04,534
Two released checkpoints specialize the input conditions.

403
00:31:05,434 --> 00:31:10,588
The FL2VA checkpoint supports text-only generation and first/last-frame-conditioned audio-video generation.

404
00:31:10,588 --> 00:31:14,971
Endpoint frames are powerful when the narrative requires a defined entrance and exit state.

405
00:31:14,971 --> 00:31:22,919
The model still invents the path between endpoints, so choose frames that are physically and compositionally compatible rather than forcing an impossible transition.

406
00:31:22,919 --> 00:31:25,664
The Ref2VA checkpoint accepts a richer reference package.

407
00:31:26,564 --> 00:31:30,197
Ref2VA accepts multimodal references within a twelve-file total.

408
00:31:30,197 --> 00:31:37,917
Images can anchor characters or style, videos can supply motion or camera language, and audio can define voice, rhythm, or event timing.

409
00:31:37,917 --> 00:31:39,791
Again, the limit is not a target.

410
00:31:39,791 --> 00:31:44,899
A smaller set with explicit roles reduces ambiguity and makes failures easier to diagnose.

411
00:31:44,899 --> 00:31:48,419
The final two K stage regenerates rather than merely enlarges.

412
00:31:49,319 --> 00:31:55,121
Conventional super-resolution mainly predicts missing high-frequency detail from a low-resolution image or video.

413
00:31:55,121 --> 00:32:03,593
H three-Regenerate-two K conditions on both the seven sixty-eight P result and the original multimodal context, allowing text and references to reassert fine detail.

414
00:32:03,593 --> 00:32:08,163
That can improve typography and brand elements, but regeneration may also change content.

415
00:32:08,163 --> 00:32:11,141
High resolution therefore needs another continuity review.

416
00:32:11,141 --> 00:32:13,914
Open deployment brings a different constraint: memory.

417
00:32:14,814 --> 00:32:21,952
The open release provides BF16 checkpoints for FL2VA and Ref2VA, with support paths through SGLang, vLLM, Diffusers, and Comfy U I.

418
00:32:21,952 --> 00:32:27,347
Sparse attention is described as native, but the first released inference path uses full attention.

419
00:32:27,347 --> 00:32:34,812
A realistic class workflow may use hosted generation or cloud nodes while using Comfy U I to make the pipeline explicit and reproducible.

420
00:32:34,812 --> 00:32:38,899
Now compare the systems without pretending they publish identical evidence.

421
00:32:39,799 --> 00:32:44,324
Seedance emphasizes polished multimodal creation and longer one-take workflows.

422
00:32:44,324 --> 00:32:47,532
H three exposes a detailed open omni-modal architecture.

423
00:32:47,532 --> 00:32:51,198
Wan offers a broad open ecosystem with strong Comfy U I support.

424
00:32:51,198 --> 00:32:57,958
Hunyuan Video 1.5 targets efficient consumer-grade inference with an 8.3B model and a cascaded super-resolution stage.

425
00:32:57,958 --> 00:33:04,832
The right model is the one whose controllable task matches the shot and whose deployment constraints fit the production.

426
00:33:04,832 --> 00:33:08,899
We can now turn architecture knowledge into a repeatable film workflow.

427
00:33:09,799 --> 00:33:13,447
Current models are shot engines, reference engines, and editing engines.

428
00:33:13,447 --> 00:33:18,971
A film still requires dramatic structure, coverage, continuity, selection, sound, rhythm, and responsibility.

429
00:33:18,971 --> 00:33:23,936
The workflow becomes reliable when each stage produces an inspectable artifact for the next stage.

430
00:33:23,936 --> 00:33:25,710
Begin before prompts, with a brief.

431
00:33:26,610 --> 00:33:28,087
The brief defines purpose.

432
00:33:28,087 --> 00:33:29,961
The world bible fixes continuity.

433
00:33:29,961 --> 00:33:32,289
The animatic tests sequence and duration.

434
00:33:32,289 --> 00:33:34,959
Shot generation produces controlled candidates.

435
00:33:34,959 --> 00:33:39,332
Post-production creates rhythm, sound, repair, color, titles, and disclosure.

436
00:33:39,332 --> 00:33:42,228
Do not move forward because a stage feels exciting.

437
00:33:42,228 --> 00:33:46,829
Move forward because its artifact answers the decisions needed by the next stage.

438
00:33:46,829 --> 00:33:48,419
Gate one is a dramatic unit.

439
00:33:49,319 --> 00:33:51,340
A useful brief can fit in four sentences.

440
00:33:51,340 --> 00:33:54,841
Name the agent, goal, change, stakes, and intended audience experience.

441
00:33:54,841 --> 00:33:59,968
Avoid beginning with visual adjectives, because style cannot rescue an event with no dramatic direction.

442
00:33:59,968 --> 00:34:05,095
‘A courier must deliver one memory before the city deletes her identity’ creates action and consequence.

443
00:34:05,095 --> 00:34:07,758
‘Cinematic cyberpunk woman’ mainly creates decoration.

444
00:34:07,758 --> 00:34:10,617
The world bible turns that brief into stable visual rules.

445
00:34:11,517 --> 00:34:14,433
The bible should identify invariants and allowed variation.

446
00:34:14,433 --> 00:34:18,338
Character silhouette and coat color may be fixed while fabric texture can vary.

447
00:34:18,338 --> 00:34:21,155
The room geometry may be fixed while light state changes.

448
00:34:21,155 --> 00:34:23,874
The camera may remain observational rather than heroic.

449
00:34:23,874 --> 00:34:27,729
Reference images are evidence for these rules, not a random inspiration board.

450
00:34:27,729 --> 00:34:29,657
Next convert story beats into coverage.

451
00:34:30,557 --> 00:34:32,464
Write the shot list before generation.

452
00:34:32,464 --> 00:34:40,193
A shot should answer why it exists in the edit, how long it needs to be, what the camera does, which action completes, and what must match adjacent shots.

453
00:34:40,193 --> 00:34:44,258
A five-second reaction shot may need stable eyeline more than spectacular motion.

454
00:34:44,258 --> 00:34:47,169
That acceptance criterion changes the generation strategy.

455
00:34:47,169 --> 00:34:50,833
Storyboard and animatic expose sequence problems while changes are cheap.

456
00:34:51,733 --> 00:34:54,839
Storyboard frames establish spatial relations and continuity.

457
00:34:54,839 --> 00:34:58,098
The animatic adds approximate timing, temporary sound, and cuts.

458
00:34:58,098 --> 00:35:03,292
It is acceptable for these materials to be rough; their job is to reveal missing geography and rhythm.

459
00:35:03,292 --> 00:35:10,980
If the audience cannot understand the action in grey boxes and temporary audio, higher-fidelity video will probably hide rather than solve the problem.

460
00:35:10,980 --> 00:35:13,170
Now choose a generation mode for each shot.

461
00:35:14,070 --> 00:35:15,581
Choose task mode shot by shot.

462
00:35:15,581 --> 00:35:18,200
Text-to-video is useful when invention is desirable.

463
00:35:18,200 --> 00:35:20,315
Image-to-video preserves a designed frame.

464
00:35:20,315 --> 00:35:22,128
First-last-frame controls endpoints.

465
00:35:22,128 --> 00:35:24,394
Reference generation anchors reusable assets.

466
00:35:24,394 --> 00:35:27,214
Video-to-video or editing preserves blocking and timing.

467
00:35:27,214 --> 00:35:32,149
The less you need the model to invent, the more controllable and reproducible the shot can become.

468
00:35:32,149 --> 00:35:35,322
Reference preparation is therefore a technical production task.

469
00:35:36,222 --> 00:35:43,469
Prepare references at sensible resolution, remove irrelevant borders or overlays, verify rights and consent, and label what each file controls.

470
00:35:43,469 --> 00:35:47,017
Check for conflicts in age, costume, lighting, viewpoint, or movement.

471
00:35:47,017 --> 00:35:52,896
A reference sheet with a one-line role under each asset is more useful than an unlabeled folder of beautiful images.

472
00:35:52,896 --> 00:35:55,430
Then write the shot instruction as a timed system.

473
00:35:56,330 --> 00:36:01,182
Begin with what must remain fixed: subject, environment, lens, lighting, and style constraints.

474
00:36:01,182 --> 00:36:03,787
Then state ordered events with simple timing words.

475
00:36:03,787 --> 00:36:08,077
End with negative or exclusion constraints only when they address observed failures.

476
00:36:08,077 --> 00:36:09,456
Prompts should be testable.

477
00:36:09,456 --> 00:36:13,593
If the clip fails, you need to know which clause controlled the missing behavior.

478
00:36:13,593 --> 00:36:16,096
Comfy U I makes this pipeline visible as a graph.

479
00:36:16,996 --> 00:36:20,326
Comfy U I represents the generation pipeline as connected nodes.

480
00:36:20,326 --> 00:36:27,768
The graph makes hidden dependencies explicit: checkpoint, text encoder, V A E, conditioning, latent dimensions, sampler, scheduler, and output.

481
00:36:27,768 --> 00:36:30,890
Workflow JSON also becomes a reproducible production record.

482
00:36:30,890 --> 00:36:39,424
The official Wan two point two templates are useful teaching examples because text-to-video, image-to-video, and first-last-frame workflows differ in visible nodes.

483
00:36:39,424 --> 00:36:41,870
Read the graph from inputs toward media output.

484
00:36:42,770 --> 00:36:44,933
The loader selects weights and precision.

485
00:36:44,933 --> 00:36:47,994
The text encoder converts language into condition vectors.

486
00:36:47,994 --> 00:36:50,316
Image or frame nodes add visual constraints.

487
00:36:50,316 --> 00:36:52,901
The sampler integrates the generative trajectory.

488
00:36:52,901 --> 00:36:54,906
The V A E decodes latents into frames.

489
00:36:54,906 --> 00:36:57,861
Video combine nodes set frame rate and container output.

490
00:36:57,861 --> 00:37:00,394
If composition is ignored, inspect conditioning.

491
00:37:00,394 --> 00:37:04,246
If detail shimmers after good latents, inspect decode or post-processing.

492
00:37:04,246 --> 00:37:08,573
If motion is chaotic, inspect model, duration, guidance, and endpoint constraints.

493
00:37:08,573 --> 00:37:10,895
Now set a controlled baseline before tuning.

494
00:37:11,795 --> 00:37:14,866
Start from an official template and recommended model settings.

495
00:37:14,866 --> 00:37:17,694
Choose a modest resolution and duration that fit hardware.

496
00:37:17,694 --> 00:37:21,497
Record frame count, frame rate, steps, guidance, sampler, scheduler, and seed.

497
00:37:21,497 --> 00:37:23,252
Run once before adding custom nodes.

498
00:37:23,252 --> 00:37:28,370
A baseline that completes is more valuable than an ambitious graph whose failure has ten possible causes.

499
00:37:28,370 --> 00:37:31,003
Controlled batches turn generation into an experiment.

500
00:37:31,903 --> 00:37:36,977
Decide what you are testing: camera strength, endpoint frame, guidance, motion reference, or prompt wording.

501
00:37:36,977 --> 00:37:38,997
Hold the other important conditions stable.

502
00:37:38,997 --> 00:37:42,897
Generate three to six candidates and write observations before choosing a favorite.

503
00:37:42,897 --> 00:37:48,394
Changing model, seed, prompt, duration, and reference at once may create variety, but it creates almost no knowledge.

504
00:37:48,394 --> 00:37:50,461
Selection now becomes an editorial decision.

505
00:37:51,361 --> 00:37:54,924
Review clips at full speed, frame by frame, and with neighbouring shots.

506
00:37:54,924 --> 00:37:56,607
Mark the usable in and out points.

507
00:37:56,607 --> 00:38:02,397
Judge whether the action completes, the identity holds, the eyeline matches, and there are enough frames for the cut.

508
00:38:02,397 --> 00:38:05,811
Log why a candidate was selected and keep one representative failure.

509
00:38:05,811 --> 00:38:09,572
The rejected batch documents the model boundary and supports later critique.

510
00:38:09,572 --> 00:38:12,195
Post-production restores control across the sequence.

511
00:38:13,095 --> 00:38:16,046
Build the picture edit before spending time on repair.

512
00:38:16,046 --> 00:38:18,506
Composite or inpaint only shots that survive.

513
00:38:18,506 --> 00:38:21,895
Design dialogue, ambience, Foley, and music at sequence level.

514
00:38:21,895 --> 00:38:27,032
Interpolate when motion needs it, upscale after editorial lock, then color and title the film.

515
00:38:27,032 --> 00:38:34,520
Recent filmmaking research describes AI as reconfiguring production roles and timing; it does not remove the need for editorial judgment.

516
00:38:34,520 --> 00:38:37,690
The final gate is quality control and responsible release.

517
00:38:38,590 --> 00:38:44,945
Watch once for story and feeling, once for continuity, once for audio synchronization, and once for technical artifacts.

518
00:38:44,945 --> 00:38:46,905
Confirm rights, consent, and credits.

519
00:38:46,905 --> 00:38:52,677
Record model names, versions, dates, prompts, references, important settings, edits, and disclosure language.

520
00:38:52,677 --> 00:38:55,378
Provenance is not paperwork added after creativity.

521
00:38:55,378 --> 00:38:59,562
It is what lets collaborators understand and responsibly stand behind the work.

522
00:38:59,562 --> 00:39:02,210
We can now return to the quiz with a complete map.

523
00:39:03,110 --> 00:39:05,918
These are the five ideas the quiz asks you to explain.

524
00:39:05,918 --> 00:39:14,549
Notice that they also summarize the lecture: what makes a world, how a generator works, why video is difficult, how to experiment, and how to critique current claims.

525
00:39:14,549 --> 00:39:16,473
Use your own example for each answer.

526
00:39:16,473 --> 00:39:20,684
A specific case demonstrates understanding better than repeating the slide title.

527
00:39:20,684 --> 00:39:23,804
One final production exercise turns the lecture into action.

528
00:39:24,704 --> 00:39:26,313
Work in pairs for a few minutes.

529
00:39:26,313 --> 00:39:27,318
Do not generate yet.

530
00:39:27,318 --> 00:39:34,206
Write an eight-second shot with one meaningful change, a camera constraint, a reference role, two ordered beats, and one acceptance test.

531
00:39:34,206 --> 00:39:41,093
Then choose whether text to video, image to video, first-last-frame, or reference-to-video gives the model the least unnecessary freedom.

532
00:39:41,093 --> 00:39:44,462
A good proposal might be visually modest but experimentally strong.

533
00:39:44,462 --> 00:39:47,126
The goal is to make the first generation informative.

534
00:39:47,126 --> 00:39:49,439
We finish by returning to the opening promise.

535
00:39:50,339 --> 00:39:52,625
A neural network learns transformations.

536
00:39:52,625 --> 00:39:54,797
C N Ns build local visual hierarchies.

537
00:39:54,797 --> 00:39:56,855
Transformers relate tokens globally.

538
00:39:56,855 --> 00:39:58,455
V A E models compress media.

539
00:39:58,455 --> 00:40:00,856
Diffusion and flow learn generative paths.

540
00:40:00,856 --> 00:40:04,285
Video systems extend those ideas across time and modalities.

541
00:40:04,285 --> 00:40:07,143
Filmmaking turns the resulting shots into meaning.

542
00:40:07,143 --> 00:40:12,459
Keep one habit from today: when an output surprises you, do not only ask whether you like it.

543
00:40:12,459 --> 00:40:17,203
Ask which decision produced it and what controlled comparison would teach you more.

544
00:40:17,203 --> 00:40:20,461
That is where technical literacy becomes creative agency.
